This commit is a cleanup, not the fix. Let me look at the actual fix. The issue is a stack-buffer-underflow READ 16. Let me look at the `get(name)` function more carefully and understand the underflow.

The key function:
```cpp
template <typename Char> format_arg get(basic_string_view<Char> name) const {
    if (!has_named_args()) return {};
    const auto& named_args =
        (is_packed() ? values_[-1] : args_[-1].value_).named_args;
    for (size_t i = 0; i < named_args.size; ++i) {
      if (named_args.data[i].name == name) return get(named_args.data[i].id);
    }
    return {};
  }
```

When `is_packed()` is true, `values_[-1]` reads the value at index -1. `values_` points to `args_ + 1` (from `arg_data::args()`). So `values_[-1]` = `args_[0]`, which is the `value` object holding `named_args` pointing to `named_args_`. This is valid when there are named args.

But wait - the issue is a stack-buffer-underflow READ 16. Let me think about the `deserialize` function:

```cpp
template <typename Context> basic_format_arg<Context> deserialize() const {
    basic_format_arg<Context> arg;
    std::memcpy(&arg, data, sizeof(basic_format_arg<Context>));
    return arg;
}
```

`data` is `char data[sizeof(basic_format_arg<buffer_context<Char>>)]`. If `sizeof(basic_format_arg<Context>)` is larger than `sizeof(basic_format_arg<buffer_context<Char>>)`, then `memcpy` reads past the end of `data`. But that's an over-read.

